  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss over x, z from −2 to 2. It is steeper along x, so overshooting shows there first: with no noise, a learning rate above 2/3 makes the x swings grow.",
    "Each step moves the ball by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, with spread 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b, like sampling b of 64 points. It is exactly zero at 64.",
    "The orange arrow is the mini-batch step the ball will take next; the grey arrow is −learning rate × the true slope. Both are drawn at 2× their true length in bowl units.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed. Reset replays the same sequence; 'New run' uses the next values in it. Each host step is one update.",
    "A run finishes after 40 steps. It is marked diverged when the loss goes above 6 (the loss at the bowl's steep edge) or the ball leaves the drawn square. The step-length readout is hidden once a run ends.",
    "The trail holds the start point plus up to 40 landing points. The ghost keeps the last run's path. Points off the bowl are drawn at its edge; the loss readout shows the true value.",
    "With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average. Small batches can make single steps go uphill."
  ]
}